--TEST--
`Core\Response::stream` writes a body one chunk at a time, in the order the chunks were written -- `rule:concurrency/a-stream-that-outlives-its-request-is-a-connection` and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Response\Stream::write
// A body written over time is still one body. Off a connection there is no cell
// to open, so the chunks reach this program's own output -- which is what makes
// the claim assertable here at all, no `.nvst` case being a request a server is
// answering. What is pinned is the *order*: a member that buffered its chunks
// and flushed them at the end would still print plausibly against one chunk.
var $sent = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $rows = Core\Response::stream("text/csv");
    $rows->write("id,name\n");
    $rows->write("1,ada\n");
    $rows->write("2,alan\n");
});
echo $sent;
echo "--\n";

// One parameter over two shapes, because a chunk of a response body is one
// thing written two ways -- unlike a WebSocket frame, whose two payload kinds
// are two things on the wire and so two members.
var $either = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $mixed = Core\Response::stream("application/octet-stream");
    $mixed->write("text");
    $mixed->write(" and bytes" as bytes);
});
echo $either, "\n";

// An empty chunk is nothing to frame and is not an error: it reaches no wire
// and the body goes on. A caller for which emptiness is a mistake refuses it in
// its own member, where the diagnostic can say which argument was empty.
var $skipped = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sparse = Core\Response::stream("text/plain; charset=utf-8");
    $sparse->write("before");
    $sparse->write("");
    $sparse->write("after");
});
echo $skipped, "\n";

// A media type with parameters is one string and not a shape: whatever the
// program spelled is what is declared, exactly as `bytes`' own parameter is.
var $parameterised = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $csv = Core\Response::stream("text/csv; charset=utf-8; header=present");
    $csv->write("ok");
});
echo $parameterised, "\n";
--EXPECT--
id,name
1,ada
2,alan
--
text and bytes
beforeafter
ok
